Schema changes fall into two categories, and the boundary between them must be clear to every developer who touches event definitions.

**Safe (backward-compatible) changes** — consumers with the old schema can still read new messages:
- Add an optional field with a default value
- Add a new event type to the topic
- Widen a numeric type (int → long)

**Breaking changes** — consumers with the old schema will fail:
- Remove a field
- Rename a field
- Change a field's type (string → int)
- Make an optional field required

The practical implementation uses Avro with Confluent Schema Registry. Each event schema is registered with the registry, and the registry enforces a configurable compatibility mode per subject.

```java
// V1 — original event
public record OrderPlacedV1(
    String orderId,
    String customerId,
    BigDecimal total
) {}

// V2 — SAFE: added optional field with default
// Old consumers ignore promoCode, new consumers can use it
public record OrderPlacedV2(
    String orderId,
    String customerId,
    BigDecimal total,
    @Nullable String promoCode  // new, optional
) {}
```

Schema Registry in `BACKWARD` compatibility mode rejects any new schema that would break consumers using the previous version. `FORWARD` compatibility protects producers — old messages must be readable by consumers using the new schema. `FULL` compatibility requires both directions. For most event-driven systems, `BACKWARD` is the right default: new producers should never break existing consumers.

Sometimes a breaking change is genuinely necessary. The `customerId` string needs to become a structured `Customer` object with ID, name, and email. No amount of optional field additions bridges this gap. The dual-write migration pattern handles this safely.

The approach has five phases: (1) Create the new event type or topic alongside the old one. (2) Update the producer to publish both old and new formats simultaneously. (3) Migrate consumers one by one to the new format, verifying each in staging. (4) Confirm all consumers have migrated — monitor that no consumer group is reading the old format. (5) Stop publishing the old format.

```java
@Service
public class OrderEventPublisher {

    public void publishOrderPlaced(Order order) {
        // Phase 2: dual-write — publish both formats
        kafka.send("order-events",
            new OrderPlacedV2(order.getId(),
                order.getCustomerId(), order.getTotal(), order.getPromoCode()));

        kafka.send("order-events-v3",
            new OrderPlacedV3(order.getId(),
                new Customer(order.getCustomerId(), order.getName(),
                    order.getEmail()),
                Money.of(order.getTotal(), order.getCurrency())));
    }
}
```

The dual-write phase is the riskiest — you're maintaining two publishing paths and must ensure they stay consistent. Keep this phase as short as possible. Track migration progress by monitoring consumer group offsets on both topics. When the old topic shows zero active consumer groups, it's safe to stop publishing.

Automate schema registration in CI/CD rather than registering manually. The `kafka-schema-registry-maven-plugin` tests compatibility during the build phase, so a breaking change fails the pipeline before reaching production. This shifts schema enforcement from runtime deserialization failures to build-time validation — you find out in CI, not at 2 AM.

`@EmbeddedKafka` spins up an in-process Kafka broker on a random port for the duration of the test class. Real Kafka client code (producers, listeners, error handlers) runs unchanged — no mocks.

```xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.kafka</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-kafka-test</artifactId>
    <scope>test</scope>
</dependency>
```

```java
@SpringBootTest
@EmbeddedKafka(
    partitions = 3,
    topics = {"orders", "orders.DLT"},
    brokerProperties = {
        "listeners=PLAINTEXT://localhost:9092",
        "port=9092"
    })
@DirtiesContext                                       // fresh broker between test classes
class OrderListenerTest {

    @Autowired private KafkaTemplate<String, OrderEvent> template;
    @Autowired private OrderRepository orderRepo;
    @Autowired private EmbeddedKafkaBroker embeddedKafka;

    @Test
    void listener_persists_order_event() throws Exception {
        OrderEvent event = new OrderEvent(UUID.randomUUID(), "alice", new BigDecimal("99.90"));

        template.send("orders", event.orderId().toString(), event).get(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS);

        // Wait for the listener to consume + persist (avoid Thread.sleep!)
        Awaitility.await()
            .atMost(Duration.ofSeconds(5))
            .untilAsserted(() ->
                assertThat(orderRepo.findById(event.orderId())).isPresent());
    }

    @Test
    void poison_message_routes_to_dlt() {
        // Send malformed payload → ErrorHandler routes to orders.DLT
        Map<String, Object> consumerProps = KafkaTestUtils.consumerProps(
            "dlt-test", "true", embeddedKafka);
        consumerProps.put(ConsumerConfig.AUTO_OFFSET_RESET_CONFIG, "earliest");
        try (Consumer<String, byte[]> dltConsumer =
                 new DefaultKafkaConsumerFactory<String, byte[]>(consumerProps,
                     new StringDeserializer(), new ByteArrayDeserializer()).createConsumer()) {

            embeddedKafka.consumeFromAnEmbeddedTopic(dltConsumer, "orders.DLT");
            template.send("orders", "bad-id", null);            // null payload triggers error

            ConsumerRecord<String, byte[]> dlt =
                KafkaTestUtils.getSingleRecord(dltConsumer, "orders.DLT", Duration.ofSeconds(5));
            assertThat(dlt).isNotNull();
        }
    }
}
```

```yaml
# Override bootstrap servers in test profile so app uses the embedded broker
spring:
  config:
    activate.on-profile: test
  kafka:
    bootstrap-servers: ${spring.embedded.kafka.brokers}
```

- `@EmbeddedKafka` runs an actual Kafka broker (KRaft or ZK depending on version) — your producer/consumer code is exercised end-to-end without external infrastructure.
- Use **Awaitility** or `CountDownLatch` instead of `Thread.sleep` to wait for the listener — sleeps are flaky and slow.
- `@DirtiesContext` rebuilds the application context (and the broker) between test classes, which is slow. Group related tests in one class to amortize the broker startup cost (~3-5 seconds).
- For higher fidelity (real protocol, real disks), use **Testcontainers** with `confluentinc/cp-kafka` — slower (10-15 s startup) but closer to production. Many teams use `@EmbeddedKafka` for unit-style tests and Testcontainers for end-to-end suites.

**Idempotent producer (covers most use cases):**

```java
@Configuration
public class KafkaProducerConfig {

    @Bean
    public ProducerFactory<String, OrderEvent> producerFactory() {
        Map<String, Object> props = new HashMap<>();
        props.put(ProducerConfig.BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS_CONFIG, "kafka1:9092,kafka2:9092,kafka3:9092");
        props.put(ProducerConfig.KEY_SERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG, StringSerializer.class);
        props.put(ProducerConfig.VALUE_SERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG, JsonSerializer.class);

        // Durability and idempotence (default true on 3.0+, set explicitly for clarity)
        props.put(ProducerConfig.ENABLE_IDEMPOTENCE_CONFIG, true);
        props.put(ProducerConfig.ACKS_CONFIG, "all");
        props.put(ProducerConfig.RETRIES_CONFIG, Integer.MAX_VALUE);
        props.put(ProducerConfig.MAX_IN_FLIGHT_REQUESTS_PER_CONNECTION, 5);
        props.put(ProducerConfig.DELIVERY_TIMEOUT_MS_CONFIG, 120_000);

        return new DefaultKafkaProducerFactory<>(props);
    }

    @Bean
    public KafkaTemplate<String, OrderEvent> kafkaTemplate(ProducerFactory<String, OrderEvent> pf) {
        return new KafkaTemplate<>(pf);
    }
}
```

**Transactional producer (atomic multi-partition writes + offset commit):**

```java
@Configuration
public class TransactionalKafkaConfig {

    @Bean
    public ProducerFactory<String, OrderEvent> txnProducerFactory() {
        Map<String, Object> props = new HashMap<>();
        props.put(ProducerConfig.BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS_CONFIG, "kafka1:9092,kafka2:9092,kafka3:9092");
        props.put(ProducerConfig.KEY_SERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG, StringSerializer.class);
        props.put(ProducerConfig.VALUE_SERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG, JsonSerializer.class);
        props.put(ProducerConfig.ENABLE_IDEMPOTENCE_CONFIG, true);
        props.put(ProducerConfig.ACKS_CONFIG, "all");

        DefaultKafkaProducerFactory<String, OrderEvent> pf = new DefaultKafkaProducerFactory<>(props);
        // The transactional.id prefix MUST be stable per logical producer instance.
        // Spring appends a per-instance suffix.
        pf.setTransactionIdPrefix("order-svc-tx-");
        return pf;
    }

    @Bean
    public KafkaTransactionManager<String, OrderEvent> kafkaTxManager(
            ProducerFactory<String, OrderEvent> pf) {
        return new KafkaTransactionManager<>(pf);
    }

    @Bean
    public KafkaTemplate<String, OrderEvent> txnTemplate(ProducerFactory<String, OrderEvent> pf) {
        return new KafkaTemplate<>(pf);
    }
}

@Service
public class OrderProcessor {

    private final KafkaTemplate<String, OrderEvent> kafka;

    public OrderProcessor(KafkaTemplate<String, OrderEvent> kafka) { this.kafka = kafka; }

    // Both sends commit atomically with the consumer offset — or all roll back.
    @KafkaListener(topics = "orders", groupId = "order-svc")
    @Transactional("kafkaTxManager")
    public void onOrder(OrderEvent in) {
        kafka.send("inventory-updates", in.orderId(), new InventoryAdjust(in));
        kafka.send("email-queue",       in.orderId(), new EmailRequest(in));
        // No explicit commit — Spring commits offset + sends inside one transaction.
    }
}
```

**Downstream consumer must use `read_committed`** to skip aborted or in-flight transactional records:

```yaml
spring:
  kafka:
    consumer:
      isolation-level: read_committed
      enable-auto-commit: false
```
